# XSH pthread_attr_init(), pthread_attr_destroy()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Only the default the standard fixes (detachstate, XSH
# pthread_attr_getdetachstate) can be compared; the others are
# implementation-defined.

assertion  XSH.pthread_attr_init.001
anchor     functions/pthread_attr_init.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_attr_init.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       pthread_attr_init() returns 0, pthread_attr_destroy() of the
           initialized object returns 0, and the destroyed object can be
           initialized again (0) and destroyed again (0).
test       t/001-init-destroy.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_attr_init.002
anchor     functions/pthread_attr_init.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_attr_getdetachstate.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       pthread_attr_init() sets every attribute to its default: on
           storage holding other bytes (all 0x00, all 0xFF, 0xA5), and on
           an object that was changed and destroyed, the detachstate
           attribute reads PTHREAD_CREATE_JOINABLE afterwards.
note       Only detachstate has a default the standard fixes.
test       t/002-defaults.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_attr_init.003
anchor     functions/pthread_attr_init.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       One attributes object used for many pthread_create() calls
           made at the same time by four threads: every call returns 0
           and each new thread runs its routine once, handing back the
           argument it was given.
note       pthread_create() failing with EAGAIN is not a verdict (the
           test is UNRESOLVED).
test       t/003-simultaneous-create.c
timeout    120
